      "text": "To evaluate \\(\\log_3 (27\\sqrt{3})\\), we start by expressing the argument of the logarithm in terms of powers of 3.\n\nFirst, we recognize that \\(27\\) can be written as a power of 3:\n\\[\n27 = 3^3\n\\]\n\nNext, we express \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) as a power of 3:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{3} = 3^{1/2}\n\\]\n\nNow, we combine these expressions to rewrite \\(27\\sqrt{3}\\):\n\\[\n27\\sqrt{3} = 3^3 \\cdot 3^{1/2}\n\\]\n\nUsing the property of exponents that states \\(a^m \\cdot a^n = a^{m+n}\\), we can combine the exponents:\n\\[\n3^3 \\cdot 3^{1/2} = 3^{3 + \\frac{1}{2}} = 3^{3.5}\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\n27\\sqrt{3} = 3^{3.5}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(\\log_3 (27\\sqrt{3})\\). Since \\(27\\sqrt{3} = 3^{3.5}\\), we can use the definition of logarithms:\n\\[\n\\log_3 (3^{3.5}) = 3.5\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{7}{2}}\n\\]",
